<Base title="Start here">
  <h1>The design system field manual</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A working reference for owning a design system: what to know by heart, what to look up, and the
    questions to ask before anyone writes code.
  </p>

  <h2>What this is for</h2>
  <p>
    Three situations bring you here:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>You need to recall something.</strong> Token tiers, ARIA patterns, versioning policy,
      contribution models. Each topic has its own page with examples and links to primary sources
      (W3C, WAI, MDN) rather than opinion.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>You're in a conversation.</strong> A PM sketches a feature, a designer shows a comp, an
      engineer asks "can we just build it?" Open the
      <a href="/checklist/">pre-code checklist</a> and walk the questions together. It covers the
      user, the domain, the surface, responsiveness, reuse, APIs, accessibility, AI interfaces, and
      trade-offs: everything to answer before coding starts.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>You need to point someone at the source.</strong> The
      <a href="/resources/">resources page</a> collects the official specs and reference systems in
      one place.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The map</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt><a href="/foundation/">Foundation</a></dt>
    <dd>What a design system is made of, its layers, principles, and how the parts relate.</dd>
    <dt><a href="/tokens/">Tokens</a></dt>
    <dd>Primitive → semantic → component tiers, naming, theming, and the W3C DTCG format.</dd>
    <dt><a href="/components/">Components</a></dt>
    <dd>Anatomy, states, API design, controlled vs uncontrolled, when a variant becomes a new component.</dd>
    <dt><a href="/patterns/">Patterns</a></dt>
    <dd>Compositions that solve recurring problems: forms, navigation, feedback, empty states, responsive behavior.</dd>
    <dt><a href="/accessibility/">Accessibility</a></dt>
    <dd>WCAG 2.2, ARIA done right, testing routines, and the numbers you keep reaching for.</dd>
    <dt><a href="/guidance/">Guidance</a></dt>
    <dd>Usage docs, content design, internationalization: the words and rules around the code.</dd>
    <dt><a href="/governance/">Governance</a></dt>
    <dd>Team models, contribution flow, versioning, deprecation, and measuring adoption.</dd>
  </dl>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      Prefer the primary source over the hot take. Where a claim can be grounded in W3C, WHATWG, or
      MDN documentation, the link is there. Where practice varies across the industry, the page says
      so instead of picking a winner.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
